---
// src/components/AchievementIslandSection.astro
//
// Same family as ComputerIslandSection.astro: one flanking
// illustration sitting behind centered-ish text, one JS-driven
// mouse-repel script, one CSS bob loop. It doesn't bleed off the
// section's edges, so positioning is plain vw/vh (same reasoning as
// ComputerIslandSection.astro — a vw/vh offset already scales
// continuously with the viewport, so there's no need for the
// clamp(mobileValue, calc(a + b*vw), desktopValue) trick
// ArtIslandSection.astro needs for its bleeding corners).
//
//   - `leftImage`  (statue island, svg) -> now sitting where the
//     temple used to (see "New: temple removed" below), right side,
//     tucked in close to the edge. The prop is still called
//     `leftImage`/`leftImageGlitched` even though it no longer
//     renders on the left — it names WHICH image this is (the
//     statue), not where it's positioned, and renaming it would mean
//     touching index.astro too for a change that was only ever about
//     position. The CSS class doing the actual positioning is
//     `.achievement-island-right` below, which DOES describe where
//     it renders.
//
// (The mountain shape bottom-left in your screenshot is the
// persistent CornerIsland.astro front island that's on every section
// via Layout.astro — not this component's own image.)
//
// New: temple removed. `rightImage`/`rightImageGlitched` (the temple
// island and its glitch swap) and `rightChunk1Image`/`rightChunk2Image`
// (its two front rock chunks) are gone entirely per your request —
// props, markup, CSS, and the JS that drove the chunks' proximity
// show/hide are all removed, not just hidden, along with the temple's
// own mobile tightened-`right` rule (its VALUES live on
// `.achievement-island-right` below now, repurposed for the statue).
// `leftImage` (the statue) moved into the temple's old spot instead
// — see `.achievement-island-right` in the styles below, which used
// to be the temple's rule and is now the statue's, position and
// animation unchanged from what the temple had, only the image and
// its size (the statue's own `clamp(130px, 12vw, 200px)`, not the
// temple's `clamp(320px, 40vw, 560px)`) are different. The statue's
// optional glitch swap (`leftImageGlitched`) still works exactly as
// before, just relocated along with the rest of the island — see
// "Glitch swap" below, now the only one of its kind in this file
// (`glitch-pop`/`glitch-scan` and the `.achievement-glitch-swap*`
// rules are all still here for it; everything `.achievement-glitch`-
// prefixed, i.e. the CHUNKS' machinery specifically, is gone, not
// reused).
//
// Background: `background-color: #2ddcfc` (flat cyan) stays exactly
// as it was — see the CSS comment on `.achievement-section` for why
// that swap happened. New: `backgroundImage` (your
// achievement_island_background_ver3.webp) is now layered ON TOP of
// that color, not instead of it — a new `.achievement-background-
// image` div, absolutely positioned to fill the section
// (`background-size: cover; background-position: bottom;`, the same
// full-bleed technique this family's OLD image backgrounds always
// used), sitting at `z-index: 0` so it paints above the section's own
// flat-color background (a plain `background-color` always paints at
// an element's own furthest-back layer regardless of any child's
// z-index, same reasoning as ComputerIslandSection's gradient) but
// below the foreground island (`z-index: 1`) and text (`z-index: 2`).
// The image itself is mostly transparent in its lower-right — sampled
// its alpha channel directly: solid/opaque from about 65% down to 87%
// of its own height (the ruins' ground plane), lighter and more
// scattered above that (the ruins structure itself, denser on the
// LEFT half of the image than the right), which is why `cover` +
// `background-position: bottom` was the right anchor choice — it
// keeps that solid ground plane flush with the section's own bottom
// edge on every viewport, the same way every other full-bleed
// background in this family anchors its own "floor" line, cropping
// any excess from the top instead of the bottom if the section ends
// up proportionally taller than the image. The transparent areas (the
// sky, and the whole right side where the statue now sits) simply let
// the flat cyan color underneath show through — that's the whole
// point of layering it instead of replacing the color.
//
// No seam-fade classes any more — the shared fade-band system that
// used to blend section edges together was removed per your request,
// so this section now just butts directly against ComputerIslandSection
// above it and BonusIslandSection below it.
//
// Still carries `.sky-chain`, so the gap-collapsing on both sides
// keeps working regardless of what backs it.
//
// Carries `.halftone-hover`, joining the single, page-wide cursor-
// spotlight element in Layout.astro (`.halftone-spotlight`, see the
// comment there) purely by having this class — no markup or script
// needed here for it, since `body:has(.halftone-hover:hover)
// .halftone-spotlight` is what actually turns the spotlight on.
//
// Glitch swap: an optional `leftImageGlitched` (your
// left_achievement_island_glitched.webp) CROSSFADES the statue from
// its normal image to this glitched variant and back on cursor
// proximity, instead of an element popping in/out of existence like
// the (now-removed) chunks used to. Implemented as two stacked images
// inside a `.achievement-glitch-swap` wrapper (same nested-stack
// technique as `.intro-cloud-inner`/`.cloud-smile` in
// IntroIslandSection.astro): the normal image sits in normal flow and
// is always visible; the glitched image is absolutely positioned on
// top of it at `opacity: 0`, and only shows (with the same
// `glitch-pop`/`glitch-scan` "digital tear" flourish the chunks used
// to share this with) while `.is-glitched` is applied. The prop is
// optional — without it the island just renders its normal image with
// no swap behavior.
//
// The proximity check driving the swap is `watchGlitchReveal()` in
// the script below — a small generic helper (watch a distance, toggle
// a class, re-glitch on an interval while in range, linger after
// leaving) that used to also drive the two front chunks' whole-element
// show/hide before they were removed; it's kept generic rather than
// hard-coded to the statue specifically, so a second island picking
// this up later needs no script changes, only the same markup
// pattern. It's discovered generically too (every
// `.achievement-glitch-swap` on the page that actually has a
// `.achievement-glitch-swap-alt` inside it, currently just the
// statue), with distance measured from its closest
// `.achievement-island` ancestor (the whole island's footprint, not
// just the swap wrapper) and a reveal radius that scales with that
// island's own rendered size.
//
// `pointer-events: none` stays on `.achievement-island` (same as every
// other island) since it's purely decorative and `aria-hidden`, so
// none of this can use native `:hover`/`mouseenter` — that requires
// the element to actually receive pointer events. The distance-based
// JS check works around that without needing anything here to be
// interactive.
//
// `.achievement-island-right` (the statue, see "New: temple removed"
// above) stays at `z-index: 1` — this matters for the halftone
// spotlight in Layout.astro: that element sits at `z-index: 0`
// specifically so everything at `z-index: 1`+ paints over it,
// untouched by its dot-pattern blend. Keeping the island at that tier
// means it's excluded from the halftone tint exactly like every other
// island in this family, rather than (accidentally) being treated as
// part of the background.
//
// New: on mobile (`max-width: 640px`) `.achievement-background-image`
// gets a crop override instead of showing uncropped edge-to-edge —
// same `min-height` + `aspect-ratio` + `object-fit: cover` technique
// EducationIslandSection already uses for its own mobile background,
// just with this image's real ratio (4044:3413) and `object-position:
// bottom` instead of `center`, to keep anchoring the ruins' ground
// plane (its opaque band, ~65-87% down the image) at the section's
// own floor the same way the base rule already does. See the CSS
// comment on that override for the full reasoning.
//
// Usage:
//   <AchievementIslandSection
//     id="section-achievements"
//     eyebrow="Island 04"
//     title="Do Eiusmod Tempor"
//     text="Nemo enim ipsam voluptatem quia voluptas sit aspernatur..."
//     leftImage="/images/left_achievement_island.svg"
//     leftImageGlitched="/images/left_achievement_island_glitched.webp"
//     backgroundImage="/images/achievement_island_background_ver3.webp"
//     cloudsBackground="/images/achievement_island_background_clouds.webp"
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  leftImage: string;
  leftImageGlitched?: string;
  backgroundImage: string;
  cloudsBackground: string;
}

const {
  id,
  eyebrow,
  title,
  text,
  leftImage,
  leftImageGlitched,
  backgroundImage,
  cloudsBackground,
} = Astro.props;
---

<section
  class="achievement-section sky-chain halftone-hover"
  id={id}
>
  <div class="achievement-clouds-bg" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="achievement-clouds-fill"></div>
    <div class="achievement-clouds-track-row">
      <div class="achievement-clouds-track">
        <img class="achievement-clouds-tile" src={cloudsBackground} alt="" />
        <img class="achievement-clouds-tile" src={cloudsBackground} alt="" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <img class="achievement-background-image" src={backgroundImage} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />

  <div class="content">
    {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
    <h2>{title}</h2>
    <p class="body-text">{text}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="achievement-island achievement-island-right" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="achievement-island-inner parallax-inner">
      <div class="achievement-glitch-swap">
        <img class="achievement-glitch-swap-base" src={leftImage} alt="" />
        {leftImageGlitched && (
          <div class="achievement-glitch-swap-alt">
            <img src={leftImageGlitched} alt="" />
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  // Same proximity-repel drift as the rest of this family, driving
  // every `.parallax-inner` element on the page. If more than one of
  // these sections lands on the same page, each ships its own copy of
  // this script and each queries ALL `.parallax-inner` elements
  // globally (not just its own) — intentional/harmless (they all
  // compute the same easing toward the same targets), just a little
  // redundant. Worth hoisting into one shared script if this family
  // keeps growing.
  const islands = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".parallax-inner");
  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
    "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
  ).matches;

  if (islands.length && !prefersReducedMotion) {
    const maxOffset = 36; // px, strongest push when cursor is right on top of the island
    const influenceRadius = 500; // px, push fades to 0 beyond this distance
    const ease = 0.08;

    let mouseX = 0;
    let mouseY = 0;
    let hasMouse = false;

    const state = Array.from(islands).map((el) => ({
      el,
      currentX: 0,
      currentY: 0,
    }));

    window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
      mouseX = e.clientX;
      mouseY = e.clientY;
      hasMouse = true;
    });

    function tick() {
      for (const s of state) {
        let targetX = 0;
        let targetY = 0;

        if (hasMouse) {
          const rect = s.el.getBoundingClientRect();
          const restX = rect.left + rect.width / 2 - s.currentX;
          const restY = rect.top + rect.height / 2 - s.currentY;

          const dx = restX - mouseX;
          const dy = restY - mouseY;
          const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;

          if (dist < influenceRadius) {
            const falloff = 1 - dist / influenceRadius;
            const nx = dx / dist;
            const ny = dy / dist;
            targetX = nx * maxOffset * falloff;
            targetY = ny * maxOffset * falloff;
          }
        }

        s.currentX += (targetX - s.currentX) * ease;
        s.currentY += (targetY - s.currentY) * ease;
        s.el.style.transform = `translate(${s.currentX.toFixed(2)}px, ${s.currentY.toFixed(2)}px)`;
      }
      requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }

    requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }

  // Shared cursor-proximity "glitch reveal" watcher — used below for
  // both the two front rock chunks (whole-element show/hide) and the
  // temple island's normal/glitched image crossfade. Both cases boil
  // down to the same shape: watch the distance from the cursor to
  // some element's static rest position, and toggle a class on some
  // (possibly different) element once that distance crosses a radius
  // — reveal immediately + keep re-glitching on a random interval
  // while in range, then linger for `hideDelay` after the cursor
  // leaves before reverting, cancelling that linger if the cursor
  // comes back in time. One shared function instead of duplicating
  // this per use, since the only real differences between the chunks
  // and the temple swap are WHICH class gets toggled, WHERE the
  // distance is measured from/to, and the reveal radius.
  //
  // `radius` can be a plain number (the two chunks: small, roughly
  // fixed-size images) or a function of the watched element's own
  // `DOMRect` (the temple island: its rendered size ranges 320-560px
  // wide across viewports via its own `clamp()`, so a single fixed px
  // radius would feel too tight at the large end or too generous at
  // the small end).
  function watchGlitchReveal(
    targets: {
      watch: HTMLElement; // element whose rest position distance is measured from
      toggle: HTMLElement; // element the class is actually added/removed on
      radius: number | ((rect: DOMRect) => number);
    }[],
    className: string,
    options: { hideDelay: number; glitchIntervalMin: number; glitchIntervalMax: number }
  ) {
    const state = targets.map((t) => ({
      ...t,
      visible: false,
      hideTimeout: null as ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null,
      glitchTimeout: null as ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null,
    }));

    // Force a reflow before re-adding the class so the one-shot
    // `glitch-pop`/`glitch-scan` CSS animations restart from frame 0
    // every time, rather than silently not restarting because the
    // class never really left the "applied" state from the browser's
    // point of view. Used both for the initial reveal and for every
    // re-glitch while something lingers on screen below.
    function playGlitch(el: HTMLElement) {
      el.classList.remove(className);
      void el.offsetWidth;
      el.classList.add(className);
    }

    // While something is visible, keep re-triggering the glitch flash
    // at random intervals — the initial reveal already plays one,
    // this just keeps it looking "unstable" for as long as it
    // lingers, instead of the animation playing once and then sitting
    // there static for the rest of `hideDelay`. Stops rescheduling
    // itself the moment it's hidden.
    function scheduleGlitch(s: (typeof state)[number]) {
      const delay =
        options.glitchIntervalMin +
        Math.random() * (options.glitchIntervalMax - options.glitchIntervalMin);
      s.glitchTimeout = setTimeout(() => {
        if (s.visible) {
          playGlitch(s.toggle);
          scheduleGlitch(s);
        }
      }, delay);
    }

    window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
      for (const s of state) {
        const rect = s.watch.getBoundingClientRect();
        const cx = rect.left + rect.width / 2;
        const cy = rect.top + rect.height / 2;
        const dist = Math.hypot(e.clientX - cx, e.clientY - cy);
        const radius = typeof s.radius === "function" ? s.radius(rect) : s.radius;
        const shouldShow = dist < radius;

        if (shouldShow) {
          // Cursor's back in range (or never left) — cancel any
          // pending hide so a brief dip out of range doesn't cut the
          // lingering period short.
          if (s.hideTimeout !== null) {
            clearTimeout(s.hideTimeout);
            s.hideTimeout = null;
          }

          if (!s.visible) {
            s.visible = true;
            playGlitch(s.toggle);
            scheduleGlitch(s);
          }
        } else if (s.visible && s.hideTimeout === null) {
          // Cursor just left range — don't hide immediately, let it
          // linger for `hideDelay` first. If the cursor comes back
          // before that fires, the branch above cancels it.
          s.hideTimeout = setTimeout(() => {
            s.visible = false;
            s.hideTimeout = null;
            if (s.glitchTimeout !== null) {
              clearTimeout(s.glitchTimeout);
              s.glitchTimeout = null;
            }
            s.toggle.classList.remove(className);
          }, options.hideDelay);
        }
      }
    });
  }

  const glitchTiming = { hideDelay: 2500, glitchIntervalMin: 500, glitchIntervalMax: 1400 };

  // New: the front rock chunks that used to be driven from here
  // (`.achievement-glitch` whole-element show/hide) are gone — see
  // the component-level comment's "New: temple removed" paragraph.
  // `glitchTiming` above is kept; the island swap below still uses it.

  // Island glitch swaps (see `.achievement-glitch-swap` in the
  // <style> below): crossfades an island's normal image to its
  // glitched variant and back, instead of showing/hiding a whole
  // element like the (now-removed) chunks used to. Handles however
  // many `.achievement-glitch-swap` instances exist — currently just
  // the statue (`leftImageGlitched`) — generically rather than
  // querying it by hand, so a second island picking this up later
  // needs no script changes, only the same markup pattern. Each
  // swap's distance is measured from its own closest `.achievement-
  // island` ancestor (the whole island's footprint, not just the swap
  // wrapper), so hovering anywhere near an island triggers it, and
  // only swaps that actually have a `.achievement-glitch-swap-alt`
  // (i.e. a glitched image was actually passed for that island) get a
  // watcher at all. Radius scales with the island's own rendered size
  // rather than a shared fixed px value (the statue is
  // `clamp(130px, 12vw, 200px)` wide). No `prefers-reduced-motion`
  // branch needed — the swap's default (no `.is-glitched`) already
  // shows its normal image, so simply not attaching a watcher leaves
  // it that way.
  if (!prefersReducedMotion) {
    const islandSwapTargets = Array.from(
      document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".achievement-glitch-swap")
    )
      .filter((swap) => swap.querySelector(".achievement-glitch-swap-alt"))
      .map((swap) => ({
        watch: swap.closest<HTMLElement>(".achievement-island") ?? swap,
        toggle: swap,
        radius: (rect: DOMRect) => Math.max(rect.width, rect.height) / 2 + 40,
      }));

    if (islandSwapTargets.length) {
      watchGlitchReveal(islandSwapTargets, "is-glitched", glitchTiming);
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  .achievement-section {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100vh;
    /* `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` matches the background image (measured at
       3840x2160) — combined with `width: 100%` and `min-height`, the
       browser uses whichever constraint needs MORE height: on a
       viewport wider-than-16:9 (mainly short/wide windows), the
       aspect-ratio wins and this section grows taller than one
       screen, showing the FULL image at its native ratio with no
       cropping — you scroll to see the rest, per your request,
       instead of `cover` shortening (cropping) the image's height to
       fit a too-short viewport. On a taller-than-16:9 viewport (e.g.
       mobile portrait), `min-height: 100vh` wins instead, so the
       section stays exactly one screen tall and `cover` crops the
       sides only — unchanged from before. `width: 100%` is required
       alongside `aspect-ratio` — without it, on the min-height-wins
       branch the browser would stretch this section's WIDTH to
       satisfy the ratio instead of its height, overflowing past the
       viewport edges. */
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* No `overflow: hidden` here — see the comment on `overflow-x:
       hidden` on `html` in Layout.astro. That handles horizontal
       clipping page-wide, so an island that needs more vertical room
       than this section provides isn't hard-clipped. */
    /* Flat color instead of an image on THIS element (see the
       top-of-file comment) — nothing to crop or position here, so
       `background-size`/`background-position`/`background-repeat`
       stay gone; a plain `background-color` always paints at this
       section's own furthest-back layer regardless of any child's
       z-index, same as ComputerIslandSection's gradient. New:
       `backgroundImage` is back, but as a SEPARATE layered element
       (`.achievement-background-image` below) rather than a property
       on this one — see that rule's own comment. */
    background-color: #2ddcfc;
  }

  /* New: `cloudsBackground` (your achievement_island_background_
     clouds.webp) — a looping clouds layer sitting BEHIND the ruins
     image below (`.achievement-background-image`), same two-tile
     `translateX` loop technique as ArtIslandSection's `.art-clouds-
     bg` / ComputerIslandSection's `.computer-clouds-bg` /
     InBetweenSection2's `.in-between-2-clouds-bg` — see any of those
     files' top-of-file comments for the full mechanics (real `<img>`
     tiles sized by `height: 100%; width: auto` rather than
     `background-image` + `cover`, since `cover` would crop each tile
     to its own box and, because that crop lands centered, both tiles
     would show the same cropped middle slice instead of their true
     left/right edges — animated `transform: translateX` from -50% to
     0% on a `width: max-content` track, looping seamlessly forever
     because the two tiles are pixel-identical and the source texture
     is already seamlessly tileable — this one measured at ~0.92
     average per-channel edge difference as delivered, well within the
     "looks seamless" range this codebase has used elsewhere, so no
     seam reprocessing was needed before wiring it up). `z-index: 0`
     keeps it above the flat-color background (which paints at its own
     furthest-back layer regardless of any child's z-index) and below
     the ruins image (`z-index: 1`).
     New: `top`/`bottom` in place of `inset: 0` — first shifted `8vh`
     per your first "a bit higher" request, then further to `20vh` per
     your follow-up that you wanted it higher still. This shifts the
     whole layer's own box UP by that amount as a unit (top edge moves
     above the section's own top edge, bottom edge moves up by the
     same amount too), same symmetric shift technique ArtIslandSection's
     moon uses to reposition without resizing — total height is
     unchanged (both edges move by the same amount), only WHERE that
     height sits shifts. It also means this layer bleeds that same
     amount above the section into ComputerIslandSection — unlike the
     ruins image, no z-index special-casing was added for that overlap
     zone (this layer stays at `z-index: 0`, so it can lose to
     Computer's own clouds there exactly like it would anywhere else);
     this wasn't asked for and is a side effect of the shift, not a
     design goal.
     New: brought back down from `20vh` to `5vh` per your follow-up to
     put the clouds AND the fill lower — since `.achievement-clouds-fill`
     (below) is what actually reads as "where the sky sits" now (see
     its own comment for why it's the piece that soaks up leftover
     space and pins the clouds row to this box's bottom edge), moving
     this WHOLE box down is what moves both of them together as one
     unit, rather than nudging the clouds row alone inside it. A
     smaller shift means less of this layer sits above the section's
     own top edge to begin with, so the box as a whole — fill and
     clouds row alike — renders lower within (and below) the section.
     New: bumped up to `10vh`, then further to `18vh` per your
     follow-up, after the row/fill bug fix (see the comment on the
     `display: flex` line above — the clouds row is genuinely
     bottom-anchored to THIS box's own bottom edge now, not just in
     theory), to move the clouds higher — since the clouds row anchors
     to this box's bottom edge, moving the whole box UP by more also
     drags that bottom edge (and the clouds sitting flush against it)
     up along with it. Adjust this value directly for more/less; it
     must stay mirrored on `bottom` below (same magnitude, opposite
     intent — see the paragraph above for why) to keep the box's
     total height constant while only its position shifts. */
  .achievement-clouds-bg {
    position: absolute;
    top: -18vh;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 18vh;
    z-index: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* New (BUG FIX): `display: flex; flex-direction: column;` were
       described in the comment below when this row/fill layout was
       introduced, but never actually written as real declarations
       here — the rule was still plain `display: block` this whole
       time, which is why `.achievement-clouds-fill` collapsed to
       `height: 0` (an empty block-level div has no content to size
       itself by) and `.achievement-clouds-track-row` never got
       pushed anywhere — it just sat in normal document flow at the
       very TOP of this box instead of being bottom-anchored, which is
       exactly "the clouds are high above where they need to be." */
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* New: per your request to fix the "still some gap on some
       screens" issue the same way EducationIslandSection's own clouds
       layer was eventually fixed (see that file's dedicated "Clouds
       background" top-of-file comment for the full history) -- this
       box is now a column flex container holding two children in DOM
       order: `.achievement-clouds-fill` first, `.achievement-clouds-
       track-row` (the actual clouds) second. `.achievement-clouds-
       track-row` sizes itself to `flex: 0 0 auto` -- exactly as tall
       as the clouds texture naturally renders at, whatever that works
       out to at the current viewport width -- and `.achievement-clouds-
       fill` takes `flex: 1 1 auto`, soaking up every bit of vertical
       space this box has left over. Because the fill comes FIRST and
       only the clouds row is fixed-size, the fill (not the clouds)
       absorbs all the slack, which pushes the clouds row down flush
       against this box's own BOTTOM edge on every single breakpoint --
       no more separately guessing a `margin-top` in vh per breakpoint
       and hoping it lands close enough (see the git history in this
       file's comments for how many rounds that took, and how a
       too-large guess on mobile once pushed the clouds tile straight
       past this box's own `overflow: hidden` and off-screen). The
       fill is painted the section's own flat sky color (see
       `.achievement-clouds-fill` below), so wherever it shows through
       above the clouds it's indistinguishable from the plain
       background -- there is no color/texture seam left to read as "a
       gap" any more, on any screen size, by construction rather than
       by tuning. */
  }

  /* Grows to fill whatever vertical space `.achievement-clouds-track-
     row` below doesn't need (`flex: 1 1 auto`) -- see the comment on
     `.achievement-clouds-bg` above for why this is what pins the
     clouds to the BOTTOM of that box instead of the top. Painted the
     same flat `#ECE4E0` as `.achievement-section`'s own background
     (see that rule's comment), so this fill is invisible against the
     sky behind it -- same idea as EducationIslandSection's own
     `.education-clouds-fill`, just cyan here instead of white to
     match THIS section's sky color instead of that one's. */
  .achievement-clouds-fill {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    background: #2ddcfc;
  }

  /* Sized to its own content only (`flex: 0 0 auto`) -- exactly as
     tall as the clouds texture renders at, at whatever the current
     viewport width works out to, never stretched to fill the rest of
     `.achievement-clouds-bg` (that's `.achievement-clouds-fill`'s job,
     above). `overflow: hidden` clips the horizontally-drifting track
     inside back down to one box-width, same defensive reasoning as
     EducationIslandSection's own `.education-clouds-track-row`. */
  .achievement-clouds-track-row {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 100%;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .achievement-clouds-track {
    display: flex;
    /* Keeps the two tile images from being flex-stretched vertically
       to match each other or the row -- same reasoning as
       EducationIslandSection's own clouds track. */
    align-items: flex-start;
    width: max-content;
    animation: achievement-clouds-drift 90s linear infinite;
  }

  /* `width: 130vw` (with `height: auto` deriving from the source
     texture's own ratio) ties this tile's rendered size to viewport
     WIDTH, the same way `.achievement-background-image` scales via
     `width: 100%; height: auto` -- see that rule's comment. This is
     what makes the clouds shrink and grow together with the screen
     instead of being pinned to a flat height regardless of how narrow
     the viewport gets (the old `height: 55%` behavior this replaced).
     `130vw` was picked to land close to the original desktop look;
     narrower breakpoints below override it with fixed pixel widths
     instead of letting it keep shrinking indefinitely, per your
     request to keep the clouds the same size across small and middle
     screens (see the `max-width: 1024px` block further down) -- adjust
     any of those values directly for a bigger/smaller look.
     No `margin-top` here any more -- vertical position is handled
     entirely by `.achievement-clouds-bg`'s flex column + fill above
     now, not by a per-breakpoint margin guess on this element. See
     the removed mobile override's own note, further down, for that
     history. */
  .achievement-clouds-tile {
    display: block;
    width: 130vw;
    height: auto;
    max-width: none;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }


  @keyframes achievement-clouds-drift {
    from {
      transform: translateX(-50%);
    }
    to {
      transform: translateX(0%);
    }
  }

  /* New: `backgroundImage` (your achievement_island_background_ver3.
     webp) layered on top of the flat color above, not replacing it —
     see the top-of-file comment for the full reasoning (in short:
     the image is mostly transparent outside the ruins/ground shape,
     so the cyan shows through everywhere it doesn't paint).
     New: a real `<img>` now, not a CSS `background-image` — per your
     follow-up that `cover` was cropping it (the image's own ratio,
     4044x3413 ≈ 1.19:1, is far more square than this section's 16:9
     box, so `cover` had to scale it up until its WIDTH matched the
     section exactly, at which point its HEIGHT overflowed by roughly
     50% — `background-position: bottom` was cropping all of that
     excess off the TOP, which is exactly the ruins' distinctive
     crossed-beam silhouette, the whole point of the image). A real
     `<img>` sized by `width: 100%; height: auto` scales to that same
     exact width with NO cropping at all — it just renders at its own
     natural (taller) height instead, and since neither this section
     nor its parent clip overflow (see the "No `overflow: hidden`
     here" comment on `.achievement-section` above — same reasoning
     applies to this image), that extra height is free to bleed
     upward past this section's own top edge into ComputerIslandSection
     above, exactly like ArtIslandSection's moon bleeds into THIS
     family's sections. `bottom: 0` (in place of `inset: 0`) is what
     anchors it there — the image's solid ground plane (measured via
     its own alpha channel: fully opaque from ~65% to ~87% down its
     height) stays flush with this section's own bottom edge on every
     viewport, and the excess grows upward instead of down, same
     anchor direction `background-position: bottom` used before, just
     without the crop.
     New: `z-index` bumped from `0` to `1` — turns out removing the
     CROP wasn't the whole fix. Neither `.achievement-section` nor
     `.computer-section` establishes its own stacking context (see
     ComputerIslandSection.astro's comments for that mechanism), so
     this image's z-index is compared DIRECTLY against Computer's own
     children in their overlap zone rather than against Computer as
     one unit. At `z-index: 0` it beat Computer's gradient (`z-index:
     -1`, `0 > -1`) but LOST to Computer's clouds (`z-index: 1`,
     `0 < 1`) — Computer's clouds layer covers its ENTIRE section,
     bottom edge included, so it was painting straight over the top of
     this bleeding image, which read as "still cut off" even though
     nothing was cropping it any more. `z-index: 1` ties with
     Computer's clouds (and islands) instead of losing outright; a tie
     breaks by DOM order, and this section comes AFTER Computer in the
     page, so it now wins — the bleed paints in front of Computer's
     clouds, which is what makes it actually visible. It's still below
     THIS section's own island (`z-index: 1` too, but DOM-later than
     this image, so it wins their own local tie) and text (`z-index:
     2`), so nothing here changes locally. It COULD, in principle, also
     win against Computer's own islands/text if the bleed ever reached
     that far up — in practice it doesn't: the excess height this
     image needs is a little under half of THIS section's own height,
     which only reaches partway into Computer's much taller box
     (130-150vh), well short of where Computer's own foreground content
     sits. */
  .achievement-background-image {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    margin: 0 auto;

    text-align: left;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #555;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #1a1a1a;
  }

  .body-text {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #444;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .achievement-island {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .achievement-island-inner img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* New: this used to be the temple island's rule (large, sitting
     lower and tucked in close to the right edge) — the temple itself
     is gone (see the top-of-file comment's "New: temple removed"),
     and the statue (`leftImage`, previously `.achievement-island-
     left`, positioned mid-left) moved into this exact spot instead.
     `top`/`animation` are the temple's OLD values, unchanged; `width`
     is the statue's own size (`clamp(130px, 12vw, 200px)`, not the
     temple's `clamp(320px, 40vw, 560px)`) — it's still the statue
     image, just relocated, not resized to match the temple's old
     footprint. `right` bumped from the temple's old `2vw`, first to
     `10vw`, then further to `18vw` per your follow-up to move the
     statue even further left — adjust this value directly for
     more/less. */
  .achievement-island-right {
    top: 16vh;
    right: 18vw;
    width: clamp(130px, 12vw, 200px);
    animation: bob-right 5.8s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes bob-right {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-8px); }
  }

  /* Island glitch swap: the normal and glitched images (currently
     just the statue's `leftImage`/`leftImageGlitched`) stacked on top
     of each other, same nested-stack technique as `.intro-cloud-
     inner`/`.cloud-smile` in IntroIslandSection.astro. `position:
     relative` so the absolutely positioned alt image lines up exactly
     over the base one. Sized entirely by the base image (normal
     document flow, `width: 100%; height: auto` via `.achievement-
     island-inner img` above) — the alt image and its wrapper just
     stretch to match via `inset: 0`. */
  .achievement-glitch-swap {
    position: relative;
  }

  .achievement-glitch-swap-alt {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .achievement-glitch-swap.is-glitched .achievement-glitch-swap-alt {
    opacity: 1;
    animation: glitch-pop 0.45s steps(1, jump-end) both;
  }

  /* Scanline/static texture — plain CSS, no image asset. Only present
     mid-flash (`.is-glitched .achievement-glitch-swap-alt::after`),
     faded in via its own one-shot `glitch-scan` animation on the same
     `steps()` snap timing as `glitch-pop` below, so the texture
     itself flickers in rather than smoothly fading over the image.
     `mix-blend-mode: overlay` lets it read as a texture ON the image
     rather than a flat translucent sheet sitting on top of it. Lives
     on its own div (`.achievement-glitch-swap-alt`) rather than a
     pseudo-element directly on an `<img>` — `<img>` is a replaced
     element and doesn't reliably support `::before`/`::after` across
     browsers. */
  .achievement-glitch-swap-alt::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4) 0px,
      rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4) 1px,
      transparent 1px,
      transparent 3px
    );
    mix-blend-mode: overlay;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .achievement-glitch-swap.is-glitched .achievement-glitch-swap-alt::after {
    animation: glitch-scan 0.45s steps(1, jump-end) both;
  }

  /* One-shot "digital tear" that plays whenever `.is-glitched` is
     freshly (re)applied by the script above: `animation-fill-mode:
     both` holds the handoff cleanly at both ends (no flash of the
     un-animated state before frame 0, no snap-back after the last
     frame), and `steps(1, jump-end)` makes the browser HOLD each
     keyframe's values for its whole segment and then jump straight to
     the next, rather than smoothly tweening `clip-path`/`filter`
     between them — that abrupt jump-cut is what actually reads as
     "glitchy" instead of "fading in oddly." Used to also be shared by
     the (now-removed) front chunks' `.is-visible` state — this
     keyframe stayed as-is when they went, only their own consumer
     rule was removed. */
  @keyframes glitch-pop {
    0% { clip-path: none; filter: none; }
    12% { clip-path: inset(8% 0 46% 0); filter: hue-rotate(25deg) saturate(2.2); }
    24% { clip-path: inset(58% 0 4% 0); filter: hue-rotate(-20deg) saturate(1.6); }
    38% { clip-path: inset(0 18% 0 0); filter: none; }
    52% { clip-path: inset(32% 0 28% 0); filter: hue-rotate(12deg); }
    68% { clip-path: inset(0 0 0 10%); filter: none; }
    84% { clip-path: inset(4% 0 4% 0); filter: hue-rotate(-8deg); }
    /* `none` here, not `inset(0 0 0 0)` — the two look identical while
       the element is sitting still, but `inset(0 0 0 0)` still
       establishes a real clip region exactly at this element's own
       box. `animation-fill-mode: both` locks whatever this 100%
       keyframe says for as long as the class stays applied (most of
       the time the glitched image is showing) — with an actual inset
       value there, that lingering clip region was cropping the inner
       image the moment the mouse-repel drift (a `transform` on the
       parent `.achievement-island-inner`) pushed it visually past
       this element's own edges. `none` removes the clip region
       entirely instead of just visually matching "no clip" at rest,
       so the drifting image is free to render past this box's edges
       same as every other island's does. */
    100% { clip-path: none; filter: none; }
  }

  @keyframes glitch-scan {
    0% { opacity: 0; }
    12% { opacity: 0.9; }
    24% { opacity: 0.2; }
    38% { opacity: 0.8; }
    52% { opacity: 0.3; }
    68% { opacity: 0.7; }
    100% { opacity: 0.45; }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .achievement-island-right {
      animation: none;
    }

    .achievement-clouds-track {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  /* A middle tier between the desktop default and the
     `max-width: 640px` mobile block below, so the floating island is
     also smaller on medium (tablet-ish) screens, not just phones.
     `1024px` is the same "wider than phone-only" breakpoint
     EducationIslandSection already uses for its own tablet-range
     override (see that file's comment on its own `max-width: 1024px`
     block). New: `140px` was wrong — the desktop base rule's `width:
     clamp(130px, 12vw, 200px)` only actually renders `12vw` when
     that's between its two bounds; at these tablet widths (641-
     1024px) `12vw` works out to roughly 77-123px, already BELOW the
     `130px` floor, so without any override at all this range was
     already rendering at a flat `130px` (the floor winning) — `140px`
     made it slightly BIGGER than that, the opposite of what was
     asked. Fixed to `100px`, genuinely smaller than the un-overridden
     `130px`, and between that and this file's mobile override further
     down (`90px`, for `640px` and under). Because the more specific
     `max-width: 640px` block comes AFTER this one in the file, phones
     still end up with its own smaller `90px` instead (later rule wins
     at equal specificity when both match) — this block only takes
     effect in the gap between the two, roughly tablet-width screens.
     Adjust `100px` directly for a bigger/smaller look on that range. */
  @media (max-width: 1024px) {
    .achievement-island-right {
      width: 100px;
    }

    /* New: the `.achievement-clouds-tile` width override (`1000px`,
       then `550px`, then `250px`) and the `.achievement-clouds-bg`
       `top`/`bottom` override (`0`/`0`) that used to live here are
       both gone. Per your screenshots -- mobile showing no visible
       clouds at all next to a desktop view where they read nicely
       against the ruins -- every round of shrinking/repositioning
       them separately for this breakpoint was chasing the wrong
       target: what you actually want is mobile matching the desktop
       look, not a smaller/differently-placed version of it. With
       both overrides gone, every screen size now uses the exact
       same `.achievement-clouds-tile` (`width: 130vw; height: auto`)
       and `.achievement-clouds-bg` (`top: -5vh; bottom: 5vh`) rules
       from the base CSS above -- literally the same size and
       position logic on mobile as on desktop, per your request. */
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .content {
      width: min(32ch, 100%);
      padding-inline: 1.5rem;
    }

    /* New: this used to be the temple's tighter-`right` mobile
       override (per the component-level rule's own comment above,
       these are its OLD values, unchanged) — now the statue's, so it
       doesn't creep under the now full-width text on narrow screens.
       The other island's (the statue's OLD mid-left) mobile
       drop-into-flow rule is gone along with that position — see the
       top-of-file comment's "New: temple removed" paragraph.
       `top` bumped from the original `0vh` to `20vh` per your later
       request to position the island lower on mobile screens —
       adjust this value directly for more/less. */
    .achievement-island-right {
      right: -6vw;
      top: 20vh;
      /* New: the base rule's `width: clamp(130px, 12vw, 200px)`
         bottoms out at its own `130px` MINIMUM on any narrow phone
         screen — 12vw of a ~375px-wide screen is only ~45px, well
         under that floor, so the clamp was always resolving to a
         flat 130px on mobile regardless of viewport width, never
         actually shrinking further. Per your request to make the
         floating island smaller specifically on mobile, this
         overrides that floor directly with a smaller flat size
         instead of trying to coax the clamp lower (the `12vw` middle
         term still won't win at these widths, so a clamp isn't doing
         anything useful here) — adjust this value directly for a
         bigger/smaller look. */
      width: 90px;
    }

    /* New: on mobile, crop `.achievement-background-image` left/right
       instead of showing it uncropped edge-to-edge. Same technique
       EducationIslandSection already uses for its own mobile
       background (see its `.education-image-wrap` + `img` pair): a
       `min-height` and an `aspect-ratio` on the same box, so whichever
       one computes taller wins. On a wide/landscape phone the
       `aspect-ratio` (this image's real 4044:3413 ratio) wins and the
       full image still shows; on a narrow/tall phone `min-height: 75vh`
       wins instead, which is shorter than the image's own ratio would
       give at that width, so `object-fit: cover` has to crop the
       excess off the SIDES to fill the box — left and right, exactly
       as asked.

       Education uses `object-position: center` since its photo has no
       preferred anchor. This image does (see the big comment on the
       base `.achievement-background-image` rule above): its alpha
       channel is fully opaque only from ~65-87% down its height (the
       ground plane the ruins sit on), so `object-position: bottom`
       keeps that ground plane anchored at the section's own bottom
       edge, matching how the base rule already anchors it there via
       `bottom: 0`, instead of `center` potentially sliding the ground
       plane up and reintroducing empty space underneath it. */
    .achievement-background-image {
      min-height: 75vh;
      aspect-ratio: 4044 / 3413;
      height: auto;
      object-fit: cover;
      object-position: bottom;
    }

    /* New: the mobile-only `margin-top` override that used to live
       here (`45vh`, then `95vh`, then `65vh` -- see the base rule's
       own history for why each of those needed adjusting) is gone.
       Per your request to fix this the same way EducationIslandSection's
       clouds layer was eventually fixed (see that file's "Clouds
       background" comment for the full story), `.achievement-clouds-bg`
       now positions the clouds with a flex column + a solid-color
       filler element instead of a hand-tuned `margin-top` guess --
       see that rule's own comment above for the mechanics. That
       technique already lands the clouds at the right spot on every
       breakpoint by construction, so no mobile-specific override is
       needed here any more. */
  }
</style>